@page "/agent-steps"

<CatHead Title="Steps and approvals">
    What an agent thought, which tools it ran, what it means to do next, and what it asks permission
    for. The person stays in charge: every step can be opened and read, and nothing that writes happens
    before somebody says yes. All of it is markup the app writes, in the app's words, with no script.
    A run that spans many steps, branches and hand-offs is a <a href="/workflow">workflow</a>.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentSteps.Reasoning" Title="Reasoning" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>details.reasoning</code> folds away the thinking behind an answer. It is a native
        <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>, so it opens from the keyboard and with scripting off;
        <code>.reasoning-chevron</code> turns as it opens, and <code>.reasoning-body</code> hangs the text
        off a rule under it. The summary says how long the agent thought, which is what most readers want
        to know without opening it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentSteps.Thinking" Title="While it thinks" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>data-state="running"</code> on a <code>details.reasoning</code> sets the words in
        <code>.reasoning-label</code> shimmering while the agent is still thinking, and
        <code>.reasoning-bar</code> holds it beside a stop button. When the thought is done the app
        removes the state and changes the words to what it took &mdash; &ldquo;Thought for 4 s, 3
        tools&rdquo;. Under reduced motion the words hold still; they still say what is happening.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentSteps.Steps" Title="Steps" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>ol.agent-steps</code> is the tool calls behind an answer, in order. Each <code>&lt;li&gt;</code>
        carries <code>data-state</code> &mdash; <code>pending</code>, <code>running</code>,
        <code>done</code> or <code>failed</code>, the same words as <a href="/collections"><code>.steps</code></a>
        &mdash; an <code>.agent-step-icon</code>, and a <code>details.agent-step</code> that opens on what
        the step sent and what came back. <code>.agent-step-meta</code> is the count and the time, on the
        end. A running step's marker is a small <a href="/orb">orb</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Show the call, not a description of it.</strong> The <code>.code-block</code> inside is
        what lets a reader check the agent's work; a failed step keeps the error in its own words.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentSteps.ToolCalls" Title="Tool calls" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>.agent-tool</code> on a <code>details.card</code> is one tool call the reader can open:
        <code>.agent-tool-icon</code>, the tool's <code>.agent-tool-name</code>, a
        <code>.badge.agent-tool-status</code> in the app's words, <code>.agent-tool-meta</code> for the
        time, and <code>.agent-tool-chevron</code>. <code>.agent-tool-body</code> holds what the call
        was sent and what came back, each a <a href="/structure"><code>.code-block</code></a> with a
        <code>.code-block-head</code>; <code>.agent-tool-error</code> marks the block that holds an error.
        Give each <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> <code>tabindex="0"</code>: a long line scrolls, and a scroller
        the keyboard cannot reach cannot be read.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>data-state</code> on the card paints the badge: <code>streaming</code> while the agent is
        still writing the input, <code>running</code>, <code>waiting</code> for approval,
        <code>approved</code>, <code>done</code>, <code>failed</code> and <code>denied</code> &mdash; the
        words <code>.agent-steps</code>, <code>.workflow</code> and <code>.agent-approval</code> already
        use. A model API names them input-streaming, approval-requested, approval-responded,
        output-available, output-error and output-denied; map them once. <strong>Write
        <code>open</code> on a failed call and on one waiting for approval</strong>, so the reader sees
        the error or the request without looking for it; a call waiting holds
        <code>.agent-approval-actions</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentSteps.Plan" Title="Plan" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>ul.agent-plan</code> is what the agent means to do and how far it got: <code>data-state</code>
        on each item is <code>done</code>, <code>current</code> or <code>skipped</code>, or nothing for a
        step still to come. Mark the current one <code>aria-current="step"</code> too. A
        <a href="/progress"><code>.progress</code></a> above it gives the same count at a glance.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentSteps.Approval" Title="Asking before it acts" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>.agent-approval</code> on a <a href="/card"><code>.card</code></a> is the agent asking
        permission. <code>.agent-approval-change</code> is the change itself &mdash; what, from, to &mdash;
        and chips under it say what kind of change it is. Approve is <code>.btn--go.btn--solid</code>, because it
        lets the action out; taking control back is <code>.btn--warn</code>; deny is quiet. The library's
        own meanings, so an approval reads the same as every other outward action in the app.
    </p>
    <p>
        Once the reader answers, <code>data-state="approved"</code> or <code>"denied"</code> swaps
        <code>.agent-approval-actions</code> for <code>.agent-approval-result</code>, which names who
        answered and when. Keep an undo there while the change can still be taken back.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentSteps.Question" Title="Asking the reader" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>.agent-approval--question</code> is the agent asking rather than asking leave: the
        approval's head, body and actions in the info colours. Each question is a
        <code>fieldset.agent-question</code> with its <code>&lt;legend&gt;</code> &mdash; one choice as
        <a href="/form-choice"><code>.choice-tiles</code></a>, several as <code>.form-check</code> boxes
        &mdash; and free text is a <code>div.agent-question</code> led by the field's
        <code>&lt;label&gt;</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        Send stays <code>disabled</code> until every question that needs an answer has one; that is the
        app's logic and the platform's state. Once sent, <code>data-state="answered"</code> swaps the
        actions for <code>.agent-approval-result</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentSteps.Queue" Title="Queue and todo" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; hover an item, or tab into it.</strong> <code>.agent-plan--todo</code> is a
        list the reader works through with the agent: the messages queued behind the one being answered,
        and the agent's todo list. A done item is struck through as well as dimmed.
        <code>.agent-plan-actions</code> at the end of an item holds its icon buttons, shown while the
        pointer is on the item or the focus is in it, and always on a touch screen. Each list is a
        <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> in an <a href="/collections"><code>.accordion</code></a>, its count in the
        summary.
    </p>
</CatExample>
